  <div class="new-box">
    <h4>▶ PELTON ARRAY ENGINE — KEY DESIGN PRINCIPLES</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>36 Pelton wheels total — 6 cylinder shafts × 6 wheels per shaft — each wheel 8 inch diameter with approximately 16 Pelton double scoop cups and 0.5 inch cup depth — 576 total cup impact points firing simultaneously</li>
      <li>Pelton cup geometry — center ridge splits incoming steam into two scoops simultaneously — steam deflects through approximately 180 degrees — near-total momentum transfer to wheel — most efficient turbine cup geometry ever designed for this application</li>
      <li>Two spider ladder manifolds — top manifold over top row — middle manifold between rows — both shoot steam straight down — gravity assists steam delivery and deflected exhaust steam falls away naturally after each cup impact — no exhaust steam interference with incoming jets</li>
      <li>Steam inlet pipes upgraded from 3/4 inch to 1 inch diameter — critical for RSR — feeding 36 Pelton wheels simultaneously requires significantly higher volumetric flow than the 2 pistons of the standard Miasma Decker build</li>
      <li>All 6 cylinder shaft outputs combine via serpentine chain belt into single output sprocket — bevel gear set turns output 90 degrees to align with rear drivetrain — transmission is the primary Phase 25 development item</li>
      <li>No reciprocating mass anywhere in this engine — pure rotation from zero RPM — RPM ceiling is determined by bearing specification and material fatigue limits not piston reversal inertia — 10,000 RPM is a conservative target for this geometry</li>
      <li>Inconel 718 for Pelton cup surfaces — nickel superalloy — maintains strength to 700°C — erosion resistant against high velocity steam droplet impact — same alloy used in jet engine turbine blades for identical erosion conditions</li>
      <li>Ceramic hybrid bearings mandatory for all 6 cylinder shafts — steel bearings will fail at 10,000+ RPM in this thermal environment — ceramic rolling elements with steel races — standard specification for high RPM industrial turbine applications</li>
      <li>Mid engine rear layout — engine sits where Lamborghini V12 would normally live — transverse mounted — naturally low center of gravity — weight over rear wheels aids traction — ideal supercar layout for high power rear wheel drive</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

